      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "In human and veterinary medicine, the injectable drugs ketamine and xylazine are mainly used in combination to induce, and then maintain general anaesthesia; they also provide pain and stress relief. Some side-effects have been reported on the auditory brainstem response, a method is therefore required to determine their concentrations in the brain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "This paper presents a method to determine nanogramme quantities of ketamine and xylazine in rat brain using liquid-liquid extraction and gas chromatography-mass spectrometry in selective ion monitoring mode. The technique requires only 0.5 g of sample, and uses xylazine d6 as an internal standard."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The method was linear between 0.86 and 34.4 μg/g of brain. Limits of quantification were 378 and 87 ng (approximately 0.76 and 0.17 μg/g of brain) for ketamine and xylazine, respectively. The reliability of the method in terms of accuracy, within-day and between-day precision was also demonstrated. For xylazine, bias and intra-day precision were good (<3.0%), as was between-day precision (<10.5%); the equivalent values for ketamine were 7%, 11.1% and 20.9%, respectively. Stability of the analytes in the matrix at -80 °C was assessed over five months; both compounds were found to be stable for at least 1 month, even at very low concentrations. The procedure was successfully applied to determine (for the first time) the in vivo brain levels of both drugs in animals following systemic administration."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "The procedure will be useful in future studies of the side-effects of these drugs, and their interactions with other compounds."
          }
        ]
      },
